				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/fluora" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6032389); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6032389&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606121149" alt="Fluora" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6032389)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/fluora" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6032389); return false;" >Fluora</a></span> <span class="odate time_1593993382 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">05 Jul 2020 23:56</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4710646">
									

<p>With relatively few exceptions, radiometric dating <em>only</em> works on biological samples that are no longer alive.</p>
<p>Radiometric dating relies closely on the ability to determine what the ratio of isotopes in a sample was when it was created. This is fairly easy for biological samples, since the amounts of carbon-12 and carbon-14 in nature (and therefore in all living organisms) is pretty much constant over time, since carbon-14 is generated continuously by cosmic rays in the upper atmosphere.</p>
<p>I mentioned that carbon dating requires dead samples because living creatures are constantly exchanging carbon with the atmosphere (mainly by eating plants and breathing), and therefore maintain pretty much constant isotope ratios during their lifespans. For real creatures, this is relatively unimportant, since their lifespans are so short, but it's worth mentioning that you wouldn't be able to radiometrically date a living, ancient creature as long as that creature continues to exchange carbon with the atmosphere.</p>
<p>Beyond organic matter, it's much more challenging to radiometrically date inorganic samples that may have had unknown isotope concentrations when they were formed, and it's out of the question for samples whose composition is highly novel or undetermined. I don't believe its mentioned usage in this article is realistic, since the composition and origin of the object are stated to be unclear and vaguely extraterrestrial. Its isotope ratios at the time of its creation are totally unknown, so while we could determine what they are in the present by analyzing samples, we wouldn't have a reference point to determine how long the thing has existed.</p>

							</div>

								
					
